{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lq8ruY1zQ4WQ2fzg5DTcfe","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\"\"\"\n     2→Cross-chapter character consensus builder.\n     3→\n     4→Merges character proposals across chapters, resolves aliases,\n     5→and produces the final CharacterMap.\n     6→\"\"\"\n     7→\n     8→import re\n     9→import hashlib\n    10→import time\n    11→from typing import Optional, Iterable\n    12→from collections import defaultdict\n    13→from difflib import SequenceMatcher\n    14→import logging\n    15→\n    16→from .models import (\n    17→    CharacterProposal,\n    18→    CharacterValidationResult,\n    19→    Character,\n    20→    CharacterMap,\n    21→    CharacterMention,\n    22→    CharacterType,\n    23→)\n    24→from .constants import are_potential_nicknames, get_nickname_variants\n    25→from ..llm import LLMClient\n    26→\n    27→logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n    28→\n    29→# Character classification prompts\n    30→CHARACTER_TYPE_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst classifying character types.\n    31→\n    32→Classify each character as one of:\n    33→- STORY: An active participant in the narrative who appears in scenes, speaks dialogue, or takes actions\n    34→- HISTORICAL: A real historical figure mentioned in passing (e.g., Napoleon, Einstein)\n    35→- REFERENCED: A fictional character from other works mentioned in passing (e.g., Hamlet, Sherlock Holmes)\n    36→\n    37→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n    38→\n    39→CHARACTER_TYPE_PROMPT = \"\"\"Classify this character's role in the narrative.\n    40→\n    41→CHARACTER: {name}\n    42→MENTION COUNT: {mention_count}\n    43→CHAPTERS PRESENT: {chapters}\n    44→SAMPLE CONTEXTS:\n    45→{contexts}\n    46→\n    47→Based on the contexts, determine if this character:\n    48→1. Actively participates (speaks, acts, appears in scenes) → STORY\n    49→2. Is a real historical figure mentioned in passing → HISTORICAL\n    50→3. Is a fictional character from other works → REFERENCED\n    51→\n    52→Return JSON:\n    53→{{\n    54→  \"character_type\": \"story\" | \"historical\" | \"referenced\",\n    55→  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n    56→  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n    57→}}\"\"\"\n    58→\n    59→# Common titles that should be ignored when comparing character names\n    60→# These are not part of the actual name but indicate social status or role\n    61→TITLES = {'mr', 'mrs', 'ms', 'miss', 'dr', 'sir', 'lady', 'lord', 'professor', 'captain', 'major'}\n    62→\n    63→# Epithet alias resolution prompt\n    64→EPITHET_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether different descriptive handles (epithets) refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n    65→\n    66→Descriptive handles are phrases like \"the creature\", \"the monster\", \"the old man\", \"the detective\".\n    67→\n    68→CRITICAL RULES:\n    69→1. Only merge epithets that CLEARLY refer to the same entity with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n    70→2. Use chapter appearances and context clues to verify they're the same character\n    71→3. Different descriptive characters (e.g., \"the old man\" in cottage vs \"the old man\" at inn) should NOT be merged\n    72→4. Generic phrases that could refer to anyone should NOT be merged together\n    73→\n    74→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n    75→\n    76→EPITHET_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n    77→\n    78→DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES (with chapter info and sample contexts):\n    79→{epithets}\n    80→\n    81→For each group of handles that refer to the SAME entity:\n    82→1. Pick the most specific/unique handle as canonical (e.g., \"the creature\" over \"the monster\")\n    83→2. List all other handles as aliases\n    84→\n    85→Return JSON array:\n    86→```json\n    87→[\n    88→  {{\"canonical\": \"the creature\", \"aliases\": [\"the monster\", \"the wretch\", \"the fiend\"]}},\n    89→  {{\"canonical\": \"the old man\", \"aliases\": []}}\n    90→]\n    91→```\n    92→\n    93→Return ONLY the JSON array. Include only handles you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n    94→\n    95→PAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n    96→\n    97→CRITICAL:\n    98→- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\n    99→- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\n   100→- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d22d227-e441-4958-ab08-fc52b629bcf5","uuid":"d8138583-c2cd-49da-830f-578f8d3b2e27","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py","content":"\"\"\"\nCross-chapter character consensus builder.\n\nMerges character proposals across chapters, resolves aliases,\nand produces the final CharacterMap.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport re\nimport hashlib\nimport time\nfrom typing import Optional, Iterable\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom difflib import SequenceMatcher\nimport logging\n\nfrom .models import (\n    CharacterProposal,\n    CharacterValidationResult,\n    Character,\n    CharacterMap,\n    CharacterMention,\n    CharacterType,\n)\nfrom .constants import are_potential_nicknames, get_nickname_variants\nfrom ..llm import LLMClient\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n# Character classification prompts\nCHARACTER_TYPE_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst classifying character types.\n\nClassify each character as one of:\n- STORY: An active participant in the narrative who appears in scenes, speaks dialogue, or takes actions\n- HISTORICAL: A real historical figure mentioned in passing (e.g., Napoleon, Einstein)\n- REFERENCED: A fictional character from other works mentioned in passing (e.g., Hamlet, Sherlock Holmes)\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nCHARACTER_TYPE_PROMPT = \"\"\"Classify this character's role in the narrative.\n\nCHARACTER: {name}\nMENTION COUNT: {mention_count}\nCHAPTERS PRESENT: {chapters}\nSAMPLE CONTEXTS:\n{contexts}\n\nBased on the contexts, determine if this character:\n1. Actively participates (speaks, acts, appears in scenes) → STORY\n2. Is a real historical figure mentioned in passing → HISTORICAL\n3. Is a fictional character from other works → REFERENCED\n\nReturn JSON:\n{{\n  \"character_type\": \"story\" | \"historical\" | \"referenced\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n}}\"\"\"\n\n# Common titles that should be ignored when comparing character names\n# These are not part of the actual name but indicate social status or role\nTITLES = {'mr', 'mrs', 'ms', 'miss', 'dr', 'sir', 'lady', 'lord', 'professor', 'captain', 'major'}\n\n# Epithet alias resolution prompt\nEPITHET_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether different descriptive handles (epithets) refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n\nDescriptive handles are phrases like \"the creature\", \"the monster\", \"the old man\", \"the detective\".\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n1. Only merge epithets that CLEARLY refer to the same entity with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n2. Use chapter appearances and context clues to verify they're the same character\n3. Different descriptive characters (e.g., \"the old man\" in cottage vs \"the old man\" at inn) should NOT be merged\n4. Generic phrases that could refer to anyone should NOT be merged together\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nEPITHET_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n\nDESCRIPTIVE HANDLES (with chapter info and sample contexts):\n{epithets}\n\nFor each group of handles that refer to the SAME entity:\n1. Pick the most specific/unique handle as canonical (e.g., \"the creature\" over \"the monster\")\n2. List all other handles as aliases\n\nReturn JSON array:\n```json\n[\n  {{\"canonical\": \"the creature\", \"aliases\": [\"the monster\", \"the wretch\", \"the fiend\"]}},\n  {{\"canonical\": \"the old man\", \"aliases\": []}}\n]\n```\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array. Include only handles you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n\nPAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n\nCRITICAL:\n- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\n- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\n- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2139}}}
