{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lf4wa9J3LLcMmsgMkWnQXz","type":"tool_result","content":"633:As the summer unfolds, Mrs. Howard becomes increasingly drawn into the tangled web of relationships that define the era’s elite. She witnesses Tom’s affair with Myrtle Wilson, a vibrant but desperate woman whose life is crushed by the very system she seeks to escape. The revelation of Gatsby’s past—his real name, James Gatz, and his rise from poverty through a series of calculated reinventions—unfolds gradually, revealing his obsessive love for Daisy, whom he has idealized since their brief romance years before. Gatsby’s dream of rekindling their relationship is tested when Daisy, under pressure from Tom, confesses she never truly loved him, shattering Gatsby’s illusion. The emotional climax occurs at the Plaza Hotel, where Gatsby’s dream is publicly dismantled, and the fatal accident involving Myrtle Wilson follows, with Daisy driving the car that kills her.\n827:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On a summer Sunday afternoon, Tom Buchanan forcibly removes the narrator from a train stopped in the desolate Valley of Ashes, where they meet Myrtle Wilson at her husband George B. Wilson’s run-down garage, which houses only a dusty Ford wreck; Tom and Myrtle arrange to meet in New York, with Myrtle taking a separate train car to avoid detection, and she purchases an Airedale dog for $10 from a man resembling John D. Rockefeller. Arriving at Myrtle’s top-floor apartment at 158th Street, they find it cluttered with oversized tapestried furniture and a blurred photograph mistakenly identified as a hen but later revealed to be Myrtle’s mother; Tom opens whisky from a locked bureau, and Myrtle calls her sister Catherine and the McKees, who arrive as she changes into a cream-coloured chiffon dress, transforming her demeanor into exaggerated hauteur. The party descends into chaos when Myrtle, emboldened by alcohol and the pretense of sophistication, repeatedly shouts Daisy’s name, prompting Tom to violently break her nose, after which the narrator, exhausted and disturbed, leaves the apartment and spends the night sleeping in the cold waiting room of Pennsylvania Station.</div>\n837:                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n998:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">On the hottest day of summer, the narrator arrives by train at the Buchanans’ house, where Gatsby has dismissed his servants and replaced them with Wolfsheim’s relatives; during a tense lunch, Daisy kisses Gatsby and declares her love in front of Tom, who becomes alarmed and later reveals he has investigated Gatsby’s past, mocking his Oxford claim while Gatsby observes Daisy’s voice is 'full of money.' The group departs for New York in two cars—Gatsby driving his own yellow vehicle, Tom taking the blue coupé—and after a heated ride, they stop at Wilson’s garage in the Valley of Ashes, where Wilson, ill and desperate, reveals he plans to take Myrtle away, while Myrtle, mistaking Jordan for Tom’s wife, watches in jealous terror under the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg. At the Plaza Hotel, Tom confronts Gatsby with accusations of bootlegging via drugstores tied to Wolfsheim, and under pressure, Daisy admits she once loved Tom, shattering Gatsby’s idealized dream; Tom orders them to leave in Gatsby’s car, and they depart in silence. Later, Myrtle rushes out of the garage at dusk and is struck and killed by a speeding yellow car that flees, witnessed by Michaelis, who is unsure of its exact color; Tom and the narrator arrive at the scene to find Myrtle’s body covered on a worktable, Wilson hysterical, and Tom urgently denies involvement, claiming his blue coupé was not the car and later breaks down in tears, calling the driver a 'God damned coward.' That night, Nick waits outside the Buchanan house and finds Gatsby lurking in the shadows, who confesses Daisy was driving the fatal car but intends to take the blame and will wait outside her window all night; Nick sees through the pantry window that Tom and Daisy are quietly together in the kitchen, already reconciled, and Gatsby stands alone in the moonlight, vigilantly watching a house where his dream has already ended.</div>\n1016:                    <span class=\"tag\">Myrtle Wilson</span>\n1031:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">The chapter unfolds across a single day and night, beginning with the narrator’s sleepless night haunted by a foghorn and his frantic rush to Gatsby’s mansion at dawn after hearing a taxi arrive, where he finds Gatsby dejected after seeing Daisy briefly appear at her window before turning off the light. Inside the vast, dusty, and neglected mansion, they search blindly for cigarettes and smoke on the open French windows of the drawing-room, during which Gatsby refuses to flee to Atlantic City or Montreal, insisting he must know Daisy’s fate, and reveals his past as a penniless officer who misled her into believing he was her social equal, binding himself to a dream she abandoned after their brief romance. Later, the narrator reflects on his own final afternoon with Daisy in a warm room filled with fire, where their silent intimacy and tender gestures—Daisy brushing her lips against his coat’s shoulder, he touching her fingers—reveal a love that eclipsed his ambitions, a moment deliberately crafted as a farewell before his departure. Meanwhile, George Wilson, shattered by Myrtle’s death, fixates on the yellow car and the dog-leash found in her bureau, declaring 'He murdered her' and later staring at the eyes of Doctor T. J. Eckleburg, muttering 'God sees everything' as he descends into religious delusion; after vanishing for hours, he is traced walking from his garage to Port Roosevelt, then to Gad’s Hill where he buys an uneaten sandwich and coffee, before arriving in West Egg by half-past two and asking for Gatsby’s house. At two o'clock, Gatsby dons his bathing suit, instructs the butler to bring calls to the pool, and carries the old pneumatic mattress alone to the pool, having stopped the chauffeur from using the damaged car; the butler waits until four a.m. for a call that never comes, signaling Gatsby’s final loss of hope. Gunshots are heard, and the narrator rushes to find Gatsby’s body floating in the pool, the mattress drifting with faint ripples, moments before the gardener discovers Wilson’s body in the grass, completing the tragedy. The narrator, later in his city office, attempts to call Gatsby four times only to find the line busy with a long-distance call from Detroit, and after a tense, abrupt phone call with Jordan Baker—who accuses him of being unfriendly and leaves for Southampton by car—he marks the 3:50 train and sits in silence, visibly unsettled.</div>\n1066:                <div class=\"chapter-summary\">In the aftermath of Gatsby’s death, his mansion is overrun by police, photographers, and reporters, while the coroner’s dismissal of Wilson as a 'madman' and Catherine’s false testimony simplify the narrative into a tale of grief-driven violence. The narrator, feeling sole responsibility for Gatsby’s isolation, desperately tries to reach Daisy and Meyer Wolfshiem—calling Information, sending a letter via butler, and waiting for a wire—but receives no response, only a disconnected long-distance call from Chicago that mistakenly believed Gatsby was alive. On the third day, Gatsby’s father, Henry C. Gatz, arrives by train from Minnesota, visibly shaken and bundled in a cheap ulster, displaying a worn photograph of the mansion and a childhood book with a handwritten self-improvement schedule, revealing Gatsby’s early ambition. Only Gatz and a Lutheran minister attend the funeral in a drizzling rain, as Klipspringer calls to request his tennis shoes and Wolfshiem refuses to come, citing fear of being 'mixed up' in death. At the cemetery, the man with owl-eyed glasses, drenched and wiping rain from his spectacles, arrives unexpectedly, murmurs 'Amen to that' after hearing 'Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,' and later calls Gatsby 'the poor son-of-a-bitch.' The narrator, reflecting on his Midwestern roots during a Christmas train ride to Chicago, realizes he and his circle—Tom, Daisy, Jordan, Gatsby—are all Westerners fundamentally unfit for the East, which he now sees as a distorted, haunting landscape like an El Greco painting. He meets Jordan Baker, who reveals she is engaged to another man; he confronts her about her dishonesty and admits he is too old to lie to himself, and they part with unresolved emotion. On a late October afternoon, Nick encounters Tom Buchanan on Fifth Avenue, who admits he told Wilson that Gatsby owned the car that killed Myrtle, justifying it as self-defense and weeping over dog biscuits left in his flat. Nick, unable to forgive him, recognizes Tom and Daisy as careless people who destroy lives and retreat into wealth; he shakes Tom’s hand, feeling as if speaking to a child, as Tom enters a jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace or cuff buttons. On his final night in New York, the narrator walks to Gatsby’s overgrown mansion, erases an obscene word scrawled on the steps with his shoe, and wanders to the beach, reflecting on Gatsby’s futile dream of reclaiming the past through Daisy, culminating in the iconic image of boats beating against the current, borne back ceaselessly into the past.</div>\n1437:                        Myrtle\n1447:                    <div class=\"aliases-list\" style=\"margin-bottom: 0.75rem;\">Also known as: George B. Wilson, George Wilson, Myrtle Wilson, George, Mrs. Wilson</div>\n1462:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Myrtle Wilson is a woman who seeks escape from her constrained life through relationships and displays of social aspiration. She exhibits a shift in demeanor when in the presence of Tom Buchanan, adopting an exaggerated sense of refinement, yet reveals vulnerability and insecurity in her personal history and relationships.</div>\n1479:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle is involved in a secret relationship with Tom Buchanan</strong></div>\n1491:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle participates in a social gathering in New York with Tom, where she adopts a refined manner</strong></div>\n1503:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle expresses frustration with servants and the lower class</strong></div>\n1515:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle claims she married George Wilson believing he was a gentleman</strong></div>\n1527:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle is present at a party where she becomes increasingly intoxicated and emotionally volatile</strong></div>\n1539:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle is physically assaulted by Tom after shouting Daisy’s name</strong></div>\n1551:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle is mistaken for being jealous of Jordan Baker</strong></div>\n1563:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>Myrtle dies in a hit-and-run accident involving a car</strong></div>\n2141:                    <div class=\"profile-body\">Michaelis is a young Greek man who operates a coffee joint near the ash-heaps and serves as a key witness to Myrtle's death. He is portrayed as an observer and occasional helper, remaining present during critical moments, offering minimal intervention, and providing testimony that shapes the narrative's factual record.</div>\n2315:                                    <div style=\"margin-top: 0.25rem;\"><strong>She makes a private, revealing observation about the strained marriages of Myrtle and Tom</strong></div>","is_error":false}]},"pa